{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 8,
  "title": "Colours and clothes",
  "description": "Name the colours, and say what you are wearing.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Colours are adjectives, and they agree with the thing they describe. The rule is the opposite of English: a colour ending in -o changes to -a for a feminine noun, and a colour that ends in -e or a consonant stays the same.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The two endings",
        "body": "Rojo becomes roja, blanco becomes blanca, but verde stays verde and azul stays azul. So you learn a colour with its ending: el vestido rojo, la camisa roja, el vestido verde, la camisa verde. If the noun is plural, the colour takes an -s: los zapatos rojos.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "el vestido rojo",
            "en": "the red dress"
          },
          {
            "es": "la camisa roja",
            "en": "the red shirt"
          },
          {
            "es": "el coche verde",
            "en": "the green car"
          },
          {
            "es": "los zapatos negros",
            "en": "the black shoes"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Saying what you are wearing",
        "body": "You use the verb llevar: llevo el abrigo. Llevar also means to carry or to bring. As with the weather, the verb carries the meaning and the clothes follow it.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "Llevo una chaqueta azul.",
            "en": "I am wearing a blue jacket."
          },
          {
            "es": "Ella lleva un sombrero negro.",
            "en": "She is wearing a black hat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Two words for the same colour",
        "body": "Your vocabulary file lists the colour as a word. In a shop you would also hear the word for the object: el rojo, el azul. The second word means the colour on its own, without a noun before it."
      }
    ]
  },
